 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Credit card rates sit about 9 to 17 percentage points above the cash rate, and they barely move when the RBA does. I couldn't get a recent personal loan rate, so I can't compare that one.\n\n**Latest (September 2026)**\n- Cash rate target: **4.36%**\n- Standard credit card (advertised): **21.24%**, a spread of about 16.9 points\n- Low-rate credit card (advertised): **13.49%**, a spread of about 9.1 points\n\n**Personal loans:** The fixed unsecured personal loan series in the RBA's F5 table stops at February 2020 (12.46%) in the data I can access, so I have no current figure. The \"personal loan\" rows that do run to 2026 are actually credit card series.\n\n**How they've moved with the cash rate**\n- **Jan 2020:** cash rate 0.75%, standard card 19.94%.\n- **2022–early 2023:** the cash rate rose from 0.10% (April 2022) to 3.10% (January 2023), but the standard card rate stayed at 19.94% until February 2023, when it edged to 19.82%.\n- **2024–2025:** the cash rate peaked at 4.35% and was cut to 3.60% by late 2025. The standard card rate kept climbing, from 20.07% to 20.99%, and the low-rate card rate went from 13.08% to 13.49%.\n- **2026:** the cash rate was lifted from 3.60% (January) to 4.36% (September). The standard card rate was unchanged at 20.99% until September, when it rose to 21.24%.\n\n**Takeaways**\n- Card rates are sticky. Since January 2020, the cash rate is up 3.6 points, while the standard card rate is up only 1.3 points.\n- The gap between cards and the cash rate has narrowed. The standard card spread was about 19 points in early 2020 and is about 17 points now, because the cash rate has risen much more than card rates.\n- Rate changes on cards tend to lag cash rate moves by months, and they are small when they come.\n\nThese are advertised rates. Rates actually paid on new loans are typically lower, and the RBA publishes those in table F6.",
